{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To determine the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) such that the number \\(4a5b32\\) is a multiple of 66, we need to ensure that \\(4a5b32\\) is divisible by both 6 and 11, since \\(66 = 6 \\times 11\\).\n\n### Step 1: Check divisibility by 6\nA number is divisible by 6 if and only if it is divisible by both 2 and 3.\n\n#### Divisibility by 2\nSince the last digit of \\(4a5b32\\) is 2, it is already divisible by 2.\n\n#### Divisibility by 3\nA number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. Let's calculate the sum of the digits of \\(4a5b32\\):\n\\[\n4 + a + 5 + b + 3 + 2 = 14 + a + b\n\\]\nFor \\(4a5b32\\) to be divisible by 3, \\(14 + a + b\\) must be divisible by 3.\n\n### Step 2: Check divisibility by 11\nA number is divisible by 11 if the alternating sum of its digits is divisible by 11. Let's calculate the alternating sum of the digits of \\(4a5b32\\):\n\\[\n4 - a + 5 - b + 3 - 2 = 10 - a - b\n\\]\nFor \\(4a5b32\\) to be divisible by 11, \\(10 - a - b\\) must be divisible by 11.\n\n### Step 3: Solve the system of congruences\nWe have two conditions:\n1. \\(14 + a + b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\)\n2. \\(10 - a - b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\\)\n\n#### Simplify the first condition\n\\[\n14 + a + b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\implies 2 + a + b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\implies a + b \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\n\n#### Simplify the second condition\n\\[\n10 - a - b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11} \\implies -a - b \\equiv -10 \\pmod{11} \\implies a + b \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1393,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,8253,279,11457,6716,1124,1188,64,11,293,10699,8,1741,429,279,1372,17767,19,64,20,65,18,17,57758,374,264,5248,315,220,21,21,11,582,1184,311,5978,429,17767,19,64,20,65,18,17,57758,374,74916,553,2176,220,21,323,220,16,16,11,2474,17767,21,21,284,220,21,1124,15136,220,16,16,59,3593,14374,14822,220,16,2